{# MagicFront chrome-only layout — PANELS. Sibling of mff-chrome-only-header/footer.html.twig,
   selected by ?mff_chrome_only=accountPanel|basketPanel|mobileMenuPanel. The three share this file:
   what changes is WHICH panel the initializer emitted, not how it is painted, and only the scoped
   one carries globals — so the three `if`s below resolve to exactly one.

   A panel is an overlay the visitor OPENS: inside a whole shop page it is invisible by construction
   (`.offcanvas` ships fixed, hidden and translated off-screen), so the merchant edited it blind. Here
   the region is painted ALONE and the offcanvas is laid flat — static, visible, untranslated — so the
   panel is simply the content of the screen. Nothing else renders: no header, no footer, no page body.

   The flattening keys on the panel's OWN widget class, never on an ancestor: every panel's script
   re-anchors its wrapper to <body> on boot (an overlay has to be a body child to cover the page), so
   a rule scoped to this container stops matching the moment the panel is ready. The style block is
   emitted here, in the body, so it always lands after the region CSS the head carries. It lays the
   panel out RELATIVE, not static: `.offcanvas` ships fixed, so the pieces the panels position
   absolutely (the close button, the empty-cart block) are laid out against the panel — turned static
   they escaped to the viewport and spread across the whole screen.

   The macro import is this template's own: Twig imports are template-local, and a block declared in a
   child does not inherit the parent's. Without it the macro call resolves to null and the panel is
   silently dropped — the screen renders white.

   The theme's `commerce-content` wrappers are not emitted either: they carry a full-viewport
   min-height, and with the panel re-anchored out of them they were left as an empty screen-tall
   spacer pushing it below the fold.

   `#mff-page-content` stays as an EMPTY, HIDDEN node for the same reason as in the other two variants:
   it is the canvas's page anchor, and leaving it visible would offer to add page content on a screen
   that does not edit pages. #}
{%- extends "Responsive/layouts/magicfront.html.twig" -%}
{%- import macrosCore.widget as widgetMacros -%}

{%- block commerceContent -%}
    <style>
        .offcanvas.widget-accountPanel,
        .offcanvas.widget-basketPanel,
        .offcanvas.widget-mobileMenuPanel {
            position: relative;
            visibility: visible;
            transform: none;
            border: 0;
            max-width: 100%;
        }
        .offcanvas-backdrop { display: none; }
    </style>
    {%- if mffAccountPanelPages|default(null) -%}
        {{ widgetMacros.widgets({ pages: mffAccountPanelPages, version: version, widgetTemplateList: mffAccountPanelTemplateList }) }}
    {%- endif -%}
    {%- if mffBasketPanelPages|default(null) -%}
        {{ widgetMacros.widgets({ pages: mffBasketPanelPages, version: version, widgetTemplateList: mffBasketPanelTemplateList }) }}
    {%- endif -%}
    {%- if mffMobileMenuPanelPages|default(null) -%}
        {{ widgetMacros.widgets({ pages: mffMobileMenuPanelPages, version: version, widgetTemplateList: mffMobileMenuPanelTemplateList }) }}
    {%- endif -%}
    <main class="main-content layout-1" hidden style="display:none">
        <div class="content-modules divisible-section"><div id="mff-page-content"></div></div>
    </main>
    <div id="lcNotify" tabindex="-1"></div>
    {%- include version ~ '/snippets/icons.html.twig' -%}
{%- endblock commerceContent -%}
